INT. ANTIQUARIAN BOOKSHOP - UPSTAIRS READING ROOM - LATE AFTERNOON

Tall shelves on three walls. A rolling ladder. Dust turning in a diagonal bar of light. A single leather armchair with a cracked arm. On a round table sit a pot of tea and two untouched cups.

PROFESSOR IMOGEN LOWERY-ACHEBE, 60s, in a tweed skirt and pearl buttons, stands by the window with her back to the room. A magnifying glass hangs from her neck on a ribbon.

Behind her, seated at the table, is her former student, DETECTIVE-SERGEANT PHILIPPA "PIP" OKONKWO-DAVIES, 30s, in a damp raincoat. She has not taken it off. Her notebook lies closed.

PIP
Thank you for seeing me.

IMOGEN
I almost didn't.

PIP
I know.

IMOGEN
You always asked questions with your hands folded. It was a trick.

PIP
It still is.

Pip unclasps her hands, deliberately, and lays them flat on the table. Imogen turns. Looks at the hands. Looks at the teapot.

IMOGEN
Shall I pour?

PIP
Please.

Imogen pours. The china clinks. A thin line of steam climbs between them.

IMOGEN
The bookseller. Mr. Fenwick.

PIP
Yes.

IMOGEN
He was found in the cellar.

PIP
Yes.

IMOGEN
With a first folio.

PIP
(a pause)
How do you know about the folio?

IMOGEN
It was in the newspaper.

PIP
The newspaper didn't mention a folio.

Imogen sets down the pot. Carefully. The spout trembles once, and she steadies it with her other hand.

IMOGEN
The newspaper mentioned a book.

PIP
A book. Yes.

IMOGEN
I assumed.

PIP
You assumed correctly.

Imogen lowers herself into the armchair, which sighs. She does not look at the shelves behind her. Pip does, once, her eye travelling along the spines to a gap at the height of Imogen's shoulder.

PIP (CONT'D)
That's a rare space.

IMOGEN
Books are borrowed.

PIP
By whom?

IMOGEN
By people who ask nicely.

PIP
And did he ask nicely?

IMOGEN
Mr. Fenwick asked in a great many ways.

Pip lifts her cup. Does not sip. Holds it, level, like a measure.

PIP
He wrote a letter to the Society. Three weeks before he died.

IMOGEN
Did he.

PIP
He said a colleague had been selling pages from the folio.

IMOGEN
Did he name the colleague?

PIP
He didn't have to.

Imogen smooths her skirt. Her fingers find the magnifying glass on its ribbon and wind the ribbon once around her hand.

IMOGEN
Philippa.

PIP
Professor.

IMOGEN
You were my best student.

PIP
I know.

IMOGEN
I taught you to see.

PIP
You did.

IMOGEN
Then see this. There is nothing in this room that is not what it appears to be.

Pip sets her cup down. Reaches into her raincoat. Draws out a slim glass sleeve, and in it, a single torn page, brown at the edges, in a looping Elizabethan hand.

PIP
Forensics found this in his hand.

Imogen looks at the page. Then at the shelf. Then at the window, where the light has slipped down the wall and is now the colour of tea.

IMOGEN
(softly)
He did write beautifully.

PIP
Who?

IMOGEN
Shakespeare. I mean Shakespeare.

PIP
I know who you mean.

They sit, two women and a pot of cooling tea, while the bar of light slides off the table and onto the floor.
